## Prerequisites and configuration

The completed application requires JDK 21, Maven 3.9+, and an independently provisioned PostgreSQL database. Supply `GENERATED_APP_DB_URL`, `GENERATED_APP_DB_USER`, and `GENERATED_APP_DB_PASSWORD` through the generated application's runtime environment or secret manager. There are no default DB passwords, JWT secrets or bootstrap credentials. Do not put secrets in source, build artifacts or Docker images. In enforced test/prod modes, `GENERATED_APP_JWT_SECRET` must be strict standard Base64 encoding of at least 32 random bytes; startup rejects missing, malformed or short values without logging them. Explicit dev mode does not instantiate the token service or require the secret. A later explicit, non-HTTP first-admin command will take operator-supplied credentials; normal startup will not create an account. The generated application needs no GSUIF database, GSUIF source files, test fixtures or OpenRouter credentials.

`prod` is the default security profile. Select exactly one of `dev`, `test`, or `prod`. `dev` permits HTTP requests and disables method security only when bound to `127.0.0.1`; an attempted non-loopback override fails startup. Test/prod deny HTTP requests until bearer HTTP integration. Both chains disable Basic and form login. No account or default login is provisioned. Credential verification uses BCrypt inside a read-only transaction; exact case-sensitive username and UUID lookups copy enabled accounts' current roles into detached immutable principals. Password hashes remain in the credential boundary. The token service issues HS256 JWTs for 15 minutes, permits 30 seconds of clock skew, and binds issuer and audience to this generated app and selected contract. It does not authorize a bearer request until the later HTTP integration reloads the account by UUID.

Hibernate uses schema validation and Flyway owns versioned migrations. No destructive reset is configured. This **partial output cannot yet start as a complete application**: login HTTP, bearer integration, first-admin bootstrap, application assembly and service implementation remain later review steps. `mvn validate` checks only the Maven scaffold model, not compilation or runtime behavior.

## Dependency choices

Spring Boot Web MVC serves the declared REST contract; Data JPA supports the app-owned entity/repository; Validation supports request constraints; Security supports the declared method roles; Spring Security JOSE provides the pinned Nimbus HS256 token codec. Flyway and its PostgreSQL adapter own future versioned migrations; the PostgreSQL driver connects only to the generated app's database. Springdoc API supplies the generated `@Operation` annotation without a Swagger UI dependency. <#if enversAudited>Hibernate Envers is included because the selected entity is audited.<#else>Hibernate Envers is omitted because the selected entity is not Envers-audited.</#if> Spring Boot `4.0.8` pins its managed dependency set; direct dependencies and build plugins have fixed versions. No version ranges, snapshots or dynamic latest selectors are used.
